{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfJFU8TXt8W8WzzUp4W8D","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The final workspace reproduces **0.30518149480962725** bit-identically, confirming the shipped state is the best-scoring one.\n\n## Final result\n\n| | Judge | knowledge | other |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Reference control | — (0.2437 public dev) | 1.9378 | 0.8859 |\n| **Shipped candidate** | **0.30518** | 1.5924 | 0.7813 |\n\n562 M params (limit 600 M), 3.8 GB peak (limit 16 GiB), 758 s inference, `question_isolation: passed`.\n\n## This round\n\n**Probe 8 — I measured the ceiling instead of guessing at it.** Rather than keep proposing conditioning schemes for the fusion layer, I computed an oracle: tuning (temperature, channel weight, floor) *per source* — impossible at inference, since the source is unknowable — moves the knowledge group only 1.6858 → 1.6670. That bounds **any** conditioning scheme at 0.94% reward, and a realisable gate would capture a fraction of it. The shipped shared-bucket calibration sits within 0.019 nats of the per-source optimum.\n\nThis retires a whole line of work, including the option-count split I'd been circling (0.0012 reward, weight pinned at the grid ceiling). The remaining knowledge error is **accuracy, not calibration** — and accuracy is bounded by a base model I can't change.\n\n**A real defect found and fixed.** I'd been claiming in the manifest and README that `python train.py` reproduces a member, but I'd only ever invoked the inner module directly through my queue scripts. Tested properly, the launcher works — 222 s, loadable checkpoint. But exercising it surfaced a genuine inconsistency: `kev/xserve.py`, the single-model serving path, was still using the **unsymmetrised, demo-free** letter channel. It isn't on the shipped ensemble's code path, so it never showed up in any score, but a single-checkpoint evaluation would have silently served the weaker channel. Now fixed and verified on both a channel-off and a channel-on checkpoint.\n\n## Where things stand\n\nAcross the run I accepted five changes (calibration, per-bucket fusion, permutation symmetrisation, anchor distillation, in-context demonstrations) and rejected eleven on measurement — several of which looked like gains until I checked whether they replicated or whether their fitted weights had run to a grid boundary. The discipline that mattered most was refusing to bank anything below the ~0.015 macro-nll standard error, and building the reliability gate (Finding 8) that later caught the cloze channel putting its weight exactly where it hadn't earned it.\n\nNotes in `NOTES.md` (8 probes, 13 findings), `EXPERIMENTS.md` (10 Judge submissions, full ablation log), `README.md` (architecture)."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":261,"cache_creation_input_tokens":432,"cache_read_input_tokens":631870,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":432},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"560ecfb8-442b-496c-96fc-76248767870b","uuid":"3347929d-4ef9-463b-bf96-63546430b307","request_id":"req_011CfJFU7APuDLh8vG8PsMdD"}
